{"author":{"id":"f4f7e123ea38957901744390924e1710825e6d55b5113472173231f66fb2d926"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781333648,"nonce":"Xban4yRcLbEU5QvjoIB48KDPpHQ=","title":"Compiler: Level 2 cross-language linking — $EXTERN FFI pragma","message":"Extend the language with a foreign-function-call pragma so compiled BASIC can call C functions directly (the natural follow-up to Level 1 --emit-obj/--main-name, shipped in 791b5a4).\n\nSyntax (apostrophe-comment pragma; interpreter ignores it, compiler picks it up):\n  '$EXTERN NAME(ARGTYPES) AS RETTYPE\n\nType mapping: INTEGER \u003c-\u003e int16_t, SINGLE \u003c-\u003e float, DOUBLE \u003c-\u003e double,\nSTRING \u003c-\u003e char * (NUL-terminated, owned by gw_str_to_cstr) or gw_string_t.\nFortran callees must use bind(c); legacy F77/F90 mangling is out of scope —\nusers write a thin C shim instead.\n\nCodegen work: parse $EXTERN decls in the analysis pass into an extern table;\nemit 'extern \u003cret\u003e NAME(\u003cargs\u003e);' prototypes in the C prologue; at expression\natom emission, disambiguate NAME(args) as an extern call (vs array access) by\nname-table lookup, emitting BASIC\u003c-\u003eC type conversions both directions.\n\nRoadmap: docs/roadmap.md 'Cross-Language Linking', Level 2.","files":null}]}